{0 The project template}

The [template/] directory is a starter Cloudflare Worker project. Copy it
outside the workero source checkout so Dune treats the copy as its own project.
Follow {{!page-getting_started}Getting started} for the complete path from a
fresh directory to a deployed Worker.

{1 Files}

- [dune-project] requires OCaml 5.4 or later and the [workero] package.
- [src/dune] builds [src/main.ml] with [(modes wasm)] and links [workero].
- [src/main.ml] registers a handler for [GET /], [POST /echo], and [GET /env].
- [wrangler.toml] points to [_worker/index.js], runs
  [workero build src/main.bc.wasm.js] on build, watches [src/], and sets
  [GREETING] in [[vars]].
- [package.json] pins Wrangler 4.151.3 and binaryen 132 as development
  dependencies. binaryen supplies [wasm-opt] for wasm_of_ocaml.
- [.gitignore] excludes build output, npm dependencies, Wrangler state, and
  local [.dev.vars] and [.env] files that may hold secrets.

{1 Run the copied project}

With the [workero] source package installed in an active opam switch, enter
your copy and install the JavaScript tools:

{@sh[
cd my-worker
npm install
npx wrangler dev
]}

Wrangler builds the worker and serves it locally, usually at
[http://127.0.0.1:8787]. In another terminal, run
[curl http://127.0.0.1:8787/]. Expect
[Hello from OCaml on Cloudflare Workers]. [GET /env] reads [GREETING] through
{!Workero.Env.var}; it returns [hello from vars] with the default configuration.

To deploy, authenticate with [npx wrangler login], then run
[npx wrangler deploy]. Wrangler prints the public [workers.dev] URL. A
Cloudflare account is needed for deployment, not for local [wrangler dev].
